**Report Format:** §2.2 Personas and Profiles
= Human Persona: Mary =

|ID| HP-01
|Name| Resident with dementia
|Status| In Progress
|Evidence basis| How this knowledge was obtained (direct observation, interview, literature, secondary data, or
proxy informant), flagged as provisional where it rests on anything other than direct contact with the
Stakeholder group it describes. For a Design Pattern, cite known uses in other projects or the literature
grounding its Principle instead.
|Elaborates| ST-01
|Role in Scenario| The person that requires the routine and the medication. Instead of being a passive recipient, they are reassured and patiently guided towards completing the task on their own terms and time. 
|Task Demands| 1. Recognizing it is time to get the medication.
2. Being reminded of the purpose of the medication without needing to ask the caregiver repeatedly
3. Having the completion of the task logged and acknowledged so it is not repeated or missed.
|Specific Capabilities and Needs| Can ask questions and agree to take the medication, communicate, and physically pick up and take the medication when reminded. Cannot reliably remember the action or its purpose when faced with external distractions. Cannot always return to a task that has been interrupted. Needs calm, friendly and simple cues and reassurance.
|Values and Motivations| (((
- HV-01: Dignity - The resident wants to be spoken to as someone capable of deciding to take their medication. 
- HV-02: Autonomy - The resident wants to feel like the task is within their control/choice, and that they can carry it out independently.
- HV-03: Privacy - The resident does not want to be constantly monitored and recorded. 
- HV-04: Wellbeing - The resident needs to take the medication in order to follow the treatment plan and maximize their own wellbeing.
)))
|Social Network and Relationships| The resident receives support from ST-02 (the care worker) and may have ST-03 (family) involved in their care. DS-02 does not change the dependency the resident has on the care worker for receiving the medication and being encouraged to take it. Instead, the frequency of that dependence is reduced, as the device is a tool that holds the routine steady in moments where the care worker may be not present or busy.
|History and Experiences| No prior technology rejection recorded. Previous experiences include repeated confusion (from PS-02), needing repeated reminders and redirection to complete tasks. Being approached repeatedly and re-explained everything can be confusing and tiring, which is why smaller, gentler cues are desirable.
|Constraints and Privacy Considerations| Will not accept being recorded generally, for privacy reasons. Monitoring should be done only to the extent strictly needed for receiving the care that is needed.   


= Human Persona: Myra =

|ID| HP-02
|Name| Care home staff member
|Status| In Progress
|Evidence basis| How this knowledge was obtained (direct observation, interview, literature, secondary data, or
proxy informant), flagged as provisional where it rests on anything other than direct contact with the
Stakeholder group it describes. For a Design Pattern, cite known uses in other projects or the literature
grounding its Principle instead.
|Elaborates| ST-02
|Role in Scenario| The professional responsible for the resident's medication being taken safely. In DS-02, their role shifts from having to personally encourage and remind the resident to take the medication and its purpose to being available on request, stepping in whenever the helper robot signals.
|Task Demands| 1. Is relieved from paying attention, in person, to several residents at the same time. 
2. Receives a signal if the situation escalates, in which case steps in and takes action personally.
3. No need for constant double checking.
|Specific Capabilities and Needs| Can talk to the patient, explain the purpose of medication and personally reassure the resident when needed. Can intervene when something is wrong. Needs the signals from the helper robot to be rare and meaningful rather than several smaller, low-value alerts.
|Values and Motivations| (((
- HV-01: Dignity - Wants the resident to be treated respectfully even when they cannot be present physically to assist them.
- HV-02: Autonomy - Lets the resident complete the activity themselves with the help of the device, rather than having to walk them through it every time.
- HV-03: Privacy - Wants to limit the surveillance at their own workplace.
- HV-04: Wellbeing - Has the resident's best interest in mind, therefore knowing the importance of medication for their general wellbeing.
)))
|Social Network and Relationships| Provides the appropriate support to the resident and, when necessary, coordinates the resident's family involvement. In DS-02, the careworker is the main figure in explaining, reassuring, and communicating with the resident's family and medical staff.
|History and Experiences| No prior technology rejection recorded. Previous experiences include the burden of needing to repeatedly return to one resident during the task. 
|Constraints and Privacy Considerations| Will not accept a device that collects and stores data in a way that could function as workplace surveillance. Will not accept a system that cannot flag genuine problems or that sends too many non-critical alerts, as this would defeat the purpose of not having to personally check the situation constantly. 


= Robot Profile: =

|ID| RP-01
|Name| 
|Status| In Progress
|Evidence basis| 
|Role in Scenario| A stationary presence when the resident takes their medication, that initiates the routine, waits, engages and confirms completion of activity. In case of non-completion, it re-engages and gently supports the resident through understanding the role and importance of the task. 
|Social Competencies| Slow, non-interruptive turn taking dialogue. Waits and holds silence before re-engaging in case of a disruption. Responds when directly addressed and delivers relevant, easy to understand information in a friendly, gentle and patient manner.
|Embodiment| A small robot with a soft light glow face and integrated speaker. No camera, no locomotion. Interaction is limited to a tray or lockable dispenser compartment with a weight or open-state sensor. The light color and intensity, as well as the voice tone are the extent of its expressivity, so as to avoid feeling coercive.
|Narrative and Identity Framing| Is a calm, patient helper within the caregiving facility. Is a tool that can talk back when prompted, designed to remind and reassure the residents of their task and importance. Avoids making medical judgments or suggesting it supervises the resident generally. It is simply a device that encourages and ensures that the resident completes the task in a warm, calm, and reassuring manner, to avoid confusing the resident further. 
|Communication Strategies| Communication through speech, using short sentences and repetitions to reduce resident's cognitive load. Warm light visual cue and speech rather than alarms or beeping. Calm, warm, and gentle tone, delivering information in a matter-of-fact kind of way. Signals by either light or vibration to an associated device owned by the caregiver in case the resident refuses to complete the activity. Sends record confirmations when the medication has been taken successfuly.
|Privacy and Data Handling| Only tray sensor, either using weight or open-closed state information, as well as the resident's yes/no answers or questions are processed. No audio or video of the surroundings is captured or retained beyond what is needed for the present interaction. Retains logs for the completion of the task for the care record. 
|Context-Aware Capabilities| Adapts timing to re-engage with respect to the time passed since the action has been initiated. Adapts escalation decision with respect to the sensor state and/or the resident's verbal confirmation. Does not build a behavioral profile of the resident over time, not adapting to the reason behind the resident's actions.
